WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.187 --> 00:00:01.130
(gentle music)

2
00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:03.680
I do want you to feel empowered.

3
00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.760
That is like the most important thing

4
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.337
out of these advice videos.

5
00:00:07.337 --> 00:00:08.877
I'm always thinking,

6
00:00:08.877 --> 00:00:11.950
"How can I communicate to a young person.

7
00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:13.970 line:15%
the power is in you?"

8
00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.553
(upbeat music)

9
00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:38.230
Hey everybody, how you guys doing?

10
00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:40.710
Welcome to the podcast.

11
00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:44.940
My guest today is the absolutely wonderful, Joanne Molinaro,

12
00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.400
aka, The Korean Vegan.

13
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:48.900
Joanne is a cookbook author.

14
00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:50.050
She's a food blogger.

15
00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:51.390
She's a marathon runner.

16
00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.570
And she's a full-time partner,

17
00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:55.620
partner in a corporate law firm,

18
00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:58.160
which is absolutely unbelievable to me.

19
00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.390
And she's got millions of fans

20
00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:02.330
across a variety of social media platforms,

21
00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:06.010
all obsessed with her wisdom-laced food content.

22
00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.840
The thing about our audience, isn't just that it's massive.

23
00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.150
It's that it's super engaged.

24
00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:12.470
And there's a reason for that

25
00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:15.550
because I think what Joanne is doing

26
00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:18.740
is essentially creating a brand new social landscape

27
00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:22.410
when it comes to food, cuisine, education,

28
00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.400
and self-improvement.

29
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.530
Her content, particularly on TikTok, is so fresh,

30
00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:31.710
so unique, so exquisitely captured,

31
00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:36.080
going way beyond just nice photos or ABC recipe prep.

32
00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:40.390
And instead is all entwined with incredibly honest,

33
00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:45.130
vulnerable, heartfelt stories about life, relationships,

34
00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:49.490
grief, family, divorce, surviving abuse,

35
00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:51.290
and the immigrant experience.

36
00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:54.350
And all of this together, more often than not,

37
00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:56.500
will leave you tearing up at the end.

38
00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:57.680
I promise you.

39
00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:59.080
She is extraordinary.

40
00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.610
Her first book, "The Korean Vegan Cookbook,"

41
00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:03.190
which is absolutely breathtaking

42
00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:05.500
and truly inverts everything you thought you knew

43
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.130
about what a cookbook is and can be.

44
00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:09.470
It comes out this week.

45
00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.410
This is one you're gonna need to pick up.

46
00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:12.850
You're gonna wanna pick up.

47
00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:16.070 line:15%
And she's here today. I could not be more excited.

48
00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:19.770 line:15%
So please hit that subscribe button and let's do this.

49
00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.203
This is me and Joanne Molinaro.

50
00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:24.730
Joanne, so nice to meet you.

51
00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:25.950
Thank you for coming out to do this.

52
00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.880
I've been dying to meet you forever.

53
00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:32.650
So it's an utter delight to have you sitting across from me.

54
00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:34.880
Well, it is a complete honor to be here.

55
00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.990
And it is not an overstatement for me to say

56
00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:39.930
that this is a complete dream come true.

57
00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:41.740 line:15%
I wanted to meet you since I went vegan.

58
00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:43.860 line:15%
So I'm very excited to be here.

59
00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.600 line:15%
We should have done this a long time ago.

60
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.020 line:15%
I think there could be no better time

61
00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:48.030 line:15%
than the present moment.

62
00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.100
Yeah, well, we're here now.

63
00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.800
It's all very exciting. You've got this book coming out.

64
00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.110
You're, you know, hitting this stride

65
00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.400
with all the stuff that you're doing.

66
00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.340
Personally, you know, I've just been super inspired

67
00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:01.910
by the work that you've been doing,

68
00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:04.060
the advocacy that you put out there,

69
00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:07.040
the message, which is this interesting, unique,

70
00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:11.220
totally compelling hybrid of self-empowerment

71
00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:15.380
and identity and food and tradition,

72
00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:18.130
like all wrapped up into these 60 second videos

73
00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:21.460
that inevitably, you know, I know you know this,

74
00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:25.540
but people, myself included, like tearing up at the end.

75
00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.930
It's just an unbelievable gift that you have

76
00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.300
for storytelling

77
00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:34.300
and basically creating an emotional connection

78
00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:37.470
between your personal experience

79
00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:39.370
and something universal

80
00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:41.760
that all of us out here can connect with.

81
00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.460
Well, I'm really glad that it's connected with you.

82
00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:48.250
And although I don't typically like making people cry.

83
00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:49.390
I do appreciate-

84
00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:50.350
You have a knack for it.

85
00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:53.395
Yeah. I think it's a talent.
What is that?

86
00:03:53.395 --> 00:03:54.228
What is that?

87
00:03:54.228 --> 00:03:57.310
Like, how do I write a script that's 60 seconds long,

88
00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:00.300
that has a three act structure

89
00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:04.376
and just sticks the landing like Simone Biles?

90
00:04:04.376 --> 00:04:07.560
You know, I've had 16 years of training.

91
00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.890
You were an appellate attorney at one point in your life.

92
00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:12.750
And we all live by word count limits.

93
00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:16.940
And 60 seconds is similar to that.

94
00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:20.810
But I want to make an emotional impact on people

95
00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:23.832
because I know that's the best way to be memorable.

96
00:04:23.832 --> 00:04:25.173
Mm-hmm. Mm-hmm.

97
00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:29.420
There's a narrative out there that you came out of the blue

98
00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:32.450
and over the past year, at the beginning of COVID,

99
00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:36.720
when you started your TikTok account and you exploded,

100
00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.920
there's this idea that this just was,

101
00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:41.930
you know, erected out of whole cloth,

102
00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:45.490
but you've been doing this since 2016, right?

103
00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:46.910
You've been at it for a while.

104
00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:48.791
You had a recent, you know, spike,

105
00:04:48.791 --> 00:04:49.624
(Joanne laughs)

106
00:04:49.624 --> 00:04:52.100
but it's not like you're new to this world

107
00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:54.170
of sharing your experience publicly.

108
00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:55.030
That's correct.

109
00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:57.647
I went vegan in 2016

110
00:04:57.647 --> 00:05:00.740
and I started The Korean Vegan at that time.

111
00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:04.500
But it isn't what it is today or it wasn't what it is today.

112
00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:07.330
At that time, it was really just a food blog,

113
00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:11.070
you know, catalog of recipes with "Oh, look, I made this,"

114
00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:12.580
and pretty pictures.

115
00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:16.810
And it wasn't until 2017 that I started to delve more

116
00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:18.690
into my personal experiences

117
00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:20.740
and start sharing more stories about my family.

118
00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:24.163
Yeah. Well, let's walk our way up to that.

119
00:05:24.163 --> 00:05:26.430
And perhaps a good place to begin.

120
00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:30.410
We'll go into your heritage and your extended family story.

121
00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:33.440
But let's start with just the impetus

122
00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:36.210
to become vegan in the first place.

123
00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:39.850
Ah, well that is a multifaceted story.

124
00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:41.930
I think there are some people who they go vegan

125
00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:44.970
because they watch one movie or they see one photograph

126
00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:49.000
or they get one bad test result from the doctor's office.

127
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.330
For me, it was a lot of different factors

128
00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:52.860
that went into that decision.

129
00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:55.647
And some of it for sure is related to your book,

130
00:05:55.647 --> 00:05:57.080
"Finding Ultra."

131
00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:00.110
My husband had just picked up a copy of your book

132
00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:03.810
and was pretty much determined to go plant-based.

133
00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:07.140
And I was very much against it.

134
00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.480
I was a paleo girl at that time.

135
00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:12.900
And I believed like many still do that,

136
00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:14.890
being vegan was unhealthy

137
00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:17.450
and that it would pretty much lead

138
00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:20.140
to all sorts of health disasters.

139
00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:23.100
And he encouraged me to read your book.

140
00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:23.933
And I did.

141
00:06:23.933 --> 00:06:25.960
I read a copy of your book.

142
00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:28.530
And I remember being incredibly inspired

143
00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:30.550
by your story about swimming

144
00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:32.760
and also leaving the practice of law.

145
00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:34.010
But then when we got to the part,

146
00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:37.110
when you went plant-based I was like, "Oh, okay."

147
00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:37.943
You lost me there.
Yeah.

148
00:06:37.943 --> 00:06:39.417
I was like, I don't agree with him.

149
00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:42.620
But Anthony was very persistent.

150
00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:44.740
And we started watching a lot of movies.

151
00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:47.800
And we watched, let's see, "Forks Over Knives,"

152
00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:49.210
We watch "Food, Inc."

153
00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:51.540
And we also watched "Cowspiracy."

154
00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:54.130
And so then these pillars of reasons

155
00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:56.447
for going plant-based started emerging, right?

156
00:06:56.447 --> 00:06:58.540
And the first one was through your book,

157
00:06:58.540 --> 00:06:59.720
you know, health, right?

158
00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:03.390
That was one of them. I wasn't sold on that quite yet.

159
00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:05.314
The second pillar-
I didn't quite do my job.

160
00:07:05.314 --> 00:07:06.470
(Joanne laughs)

161
00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:10.243
You did probably far more than I realized at that time.

162
00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:13.660
And then the second pillar was, of course, the environment,

163
00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:16.360
which was, you know, raised by "Cowspiracy."

164
00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:19.023
That was incredibly compelling to me.

165
00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:23.930
But I think sort of the third factor for me was,

166
00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:25.540
well, I had this sort of,

167
00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:28.200
you know, relationship with Anthony at the time.

168
00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:31.620
And I was worried that if I didn't join him

169
00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:33.360
in this adventure,

170
00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:37.040
that somehow something would become a wedge between us.

171
00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:40.560
And I was like, "Well, what's the harm in just trying?

172
00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.970
like, it's not like I'm getting married to veganism.

173
00:07:43.970 --> 00:07:46.570
I can just try it and see what happens."

174
00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:50.510
And that was sort of the attitude I had.

175
00:07:50.510 --> 00:07:52.400
So I did try it for a couple of weeks.

176
00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:57.400
And my father who was in perfect health, I thought,

177
00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:02.040
ultimately was diagnosed with prostate cancer.

178
00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:04.950
And after watching "Forks Over Knives"

179
00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:08.540
and seeing "The China Study" and the impact of red meat

180
00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:11.370
on East-Asian men and prostate cancer,

181
00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:15.640
I just felt it was too much of a sign to ignore if you will.

182
00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:17.700
So all of these things sort of converged

183
00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:19.960
for me to just say, "All right, I'm gonna do this.

184
00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:21.360
See what happens."

185
00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:24.990
And it was a heck of a lot easier than I ever imagined.

186
00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:27.200
And the benefits that came with it,

187
00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:31.200
completely outweighed any negatives or cons

188
00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:33.620
that I thought would adhere to the decision.

189
00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:37.600
So you basically made a pretty clean break then?

190
00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:42.300
Well, so to be fair, I think we still ate like cheese

191
00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:44.500
and butter for like three weeks.

192
00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:45.488
And then after that,

193
00:08:45.488 --> 00:08:46.772
we're like, "No, we're done." (chuckles)

194
00:08:46.772 --> 00:08:47.970
Yeah.

195
00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:51.140
So we went like probably vegetarian for about three weeks,

196
00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:52.600
and then we went vegan thereafter.

197
00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:56.024
Did Anthony do it ahead of you, and then you caught up

198
00:08:56.024 --> 00:08:58.150
or did you guys do it in solidarity together?

199
00:08:58.150 --> 00:08:59.490
We were not solidarity.

200
00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:03.840
I remember distinctly a terribly horrible fight (laughs)

201
00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:05.240
we had about it.

202
00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:06.410
He was so determined.

203
00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:07.440
And I was like, "I don't even think you know

204
00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.080
what veganism is." (laughs)

205
00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:11.580
We had a huge fight about it.

206
00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:13.840
So I was probably about two weeks behind him.

207
00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:14.800
Right. Right.

208
00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:18.910
So I'd rather let go of meat and stay with this guy,

209
00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:22.270
then continue to eat meat and jeopardize the relationship?

210
00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:24.860
Perfectly. Perfectly paraphrased.

211
00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:29.860
But what complicates this is also this cultural tradition

212
00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:36.240
amongst Koreans and the relationship to meat

213
00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:39.660
that's born out of, you know, a generation

214
00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:41.200
or two generations of people

215
00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:43.460
that were reared in essentially,

216
00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:46.780
you know, war-torn situations where people were starving.

217
00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:50.610
So, the idea of letting go of meat is anathema

218
00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:54.460
because it's so heavily associated with survival

219
00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:57.850
and that becomes woven into the very culture itself.

220
00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:00.580
So there's a threat to your heritage

221
00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:04.170
by simply making this, seemingly on the surface,

222
00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:05.760
you know, now we're in California,

223
00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:07.350
like it's just the thing to do,

224
00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:09.370
but it's a really loaded thing for you.

225
00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:10.590
It was.

226
00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:13.080
And you could not have put it better

227
00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:15.930
in terms of even just my own family's reaction

228
00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:19.350
to my decision to adopt a plant-based diet.

229
00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:21.860
But originally I was very scared

230
00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:24.504
that I would lose my identity.

231
00:10:24.504 --> 00:10:25.337
Yeah.

232
00:10:25.337 --> 00:10:26.980
And that's why The Korean Vegan was started

233
00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:29.200
'cause I was so determined not to let that happen.

234
00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:33.230
Yeah. Anybody who's visited Seoul or Korea,

235
00:10:33.230 --> 00:10:35.590
who's trying to be vegan while visiting

236
00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:37.420
can speak to the challenges of that, right?

237
00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:40.180
Like meat just finds its way into essentially everything.

238
00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:41.250
Meat or fish.
Yeah.

239
00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:42.330
It's very difficult

240
00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:44.880
or, you know, butter and dairy nowadays,

241
00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:48.940
because like me, that sort of was a sign

242
00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:49.920
of, oh, you've made it,

243
00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:52.550
you've, you know, across to the side of survival,

244
00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:54.580
wealth and indulgence.

245
00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:58.580
But is that not a modern, you know, kind of incarnation

246
00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:00.193
of the culture, right?

247
00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:02.380
It wasn't always the case.

248
00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:05.993
And like, my mind goes to Jeong Kwan.

249
00:11:05.993 --> 00:11:07.010
is that how you say it?

250
00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:08.292
I'm sure I'm butchering the pronunciation.

251
00:11:08.292 --> 00:11:09.270
Jeong Kwan Sunim.
(Joanne laughs)

252
00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:10.740
Yeah.

253
00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:13.010
Who I first became familiar with

254
00:11:13.010 --> 00:11:15.480
through my friend, Jeff Gordinier, visiting her

255
00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:17.170
and writing about her for The New York Times

256
00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:19.170
and then Chef's Table and all of that.

257
00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:22.160
What I didn't know is that you actually visited with her.

258
00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:27.160
I did, it was one of the highlights of our trip in 2019.

259
00:11:27.310 --> 00:11:30.320
My sister-in-law's mother

260
00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:34.320
is very steeped in the Buddhist community there in Korea.

261
00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:36.410
And, you know, for my birthday,

262
00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:40.230
she was able to arrange a visit with Jeong Kwan Sunim.

263
00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:43.770
And you're right, I mean, Buddhist cuisine,

264
00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:48.090
at least in Korea is largely vegetarian or vegan.

265
00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:52.280
And so vegan cuisine in Korea has been around

266
00:11:52.280 --> 00:11:56.150
quite a bit longer than bakeries and Korean barbecue.

267
00:11:56.150 --> 00:11:57.460
Right.
But right now,

268
00:11:57.460 --> 00:11:59.560
many people still associate Korean cuisine

269
00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:01.210
with, you know, grilled meat

270
00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:04.170
or, you know, these beautiful confectioneries.

271
00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:05.780
Right. Temple cuisine, right?

272
00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:07.520
Isn't that what they call it?
Temple cuisine. Yeah.

273
00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:08.353
It's wonderful.

274
00:12:08.353 --> 00:12:11.820
We actually were able to visit a number of restaurants,

275
00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:13.350
including Michelin starred restaurants

276
00:12:13.350 --> 00:12:16.930
that serve temple cuisine in Seoul and around Korea.

277
00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:21.930
And Jeong Kwan Sunim herself is a remarkably strong woman.

278
00:12:22.790 --> 00:12:24.500
And I was like such a nerd.

279
00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:26.730
I was like, so like excited to see her.

280
00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:27.990
And I'm like pulling out my phone

281
00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:30.500
and I'm like, "Look at my Instagram, I'm The Korean Vegan."

282
00:12:30.500 --> 00:12:31.620
And she's like, "Okay."

283
00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:34.578
That's cute. We've been doing this for thousands of years.

284
00:12:34.578 --> 00:12:36.080
But you know, you go girl.
Yes!

285
00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:38.300
It was kind of her thing,

286
00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:41.870
but she has such a unique philosophy on veganism.

287
00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:44.520
I think in the United States, certainly,

288
00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:47.360
and certain other Western countries,

289
00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:49.550
veganism has sort of this label,

290
00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:52.350
whether you view it as a bad label or as a good label,

291
00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:55.070
you're part of a club or you're outside of the club.

292
00:12:55.070 --> 00:12:56.177
But for her, she's like,

293
00:12:56.177 --> 00:13:00.060
"There's no such thing as vegan or non veganism in my life.

294
00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:04.100
The way I eat is merely an extrapolation of my philosophy

295
00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:05.520
and how I view the world.

296
00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:07.513
I don't think of myself as a vegan.

297
00:13:07.513 --> 00:13:09.240
I just think of myself as a human,

298
00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:12.380
who's trying to do the least amount of harm as possible."

299
00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:13.213
Right.

300
00:13:13.213 --> 00:13:16.240
It's rooted in this idea of Ahimsa, how to live your life

301
00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:18.261
and produce the least amount of harm.

302
00:13:18.261 --> 00:13:19.111
Exactly.
Yeah.

303
00:13:19.111 --> 00:13:19.944
Exactly.

304
00:13:19.944 --> 00:13:21.750
Which is a really beautiful ethos.

305
00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:23.100
It's absolutely beautiful.

306
00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:25.440
And, you know, I spent about an hour with her

307
00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:27.820
and just having that conversation with her,

308
00:13:27.820 --> 00:13:30.550
you know, I can't say it like completely changed everything

309
00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:31.860
that I view about veganism,

310
00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:34.970
but it really made me sort of understand

311
00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:37.160
that veganism is not black and white,

312
00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:39.500
that so much of it is on a spectrum.

313
00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:42.730
And that it's really counterproductive

314
00:13:42.730 --> 00:13:45.370
to try and view it as sort of an us versus them.

315
00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:47.100
You're in the club. You're not in the club.

316
00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:49.170
And it really should be rooted in, again,

317
00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:51.470
this idea, this beautiful idea that she shared

318
00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:54.570
with just try to do as little harm as you can

319
00:13:54.570 --> 00:13:55.403
while you're here.

320
00:13:55.403 --> 00:13:56.236
Right.

321
00:13:56.236 --> 00:14:00.240
'Cause fundamentally behind that is the idea of empathy

322
00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:02.000
and compassion, right?

323
00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:04.870
Which is kind of the cornerstone

324
00:14:04.870 --> 00:14:07.730
or touchdown of all the videos that you make.

325
00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:09.060
It is.

326
00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:12.280
You know, I was chatting with my therapist a few years ago

327
00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:14.510
and she's like, "What is the thing that you value?

328
00:14:14.510 --> 00:14:16.750
What do you admire most in other people?"

329
00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:21.030
And I said, "My biggest value is compassion."

330
00:14:21.030 --> 00:14:26.030
I feel like it is one of the most underrated characteristics

331
00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:27.510
in the world.

332
00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:31.410
And I feel like people think that compassion is weakness

333
00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:34.200
or compassion is crying or too much empathy.

334
00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:36.440
And for me, compassion is strength.

335
00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:38.750
It's the strongest thing that you could do

336
00:14:38.750 --> 00:14:40.690
is to be compassionate.

337
00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:44.800
So fundamentally, The Korean Vegan in its current version,

338
00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:47.863
is about compassion in every way.

339
00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:53.940
Back to this idea of Korea's relationship with cuisine.

340
00:14:55.550 --> 00:14:56.930
It seems like on some level,

341
00:14:56.930 --> 00:14:58.490
your grandmother understood this,

342
00:14:58.490 --> 00:15:01.690
the one who planted the garden in the backyard.

343
00:15:01.690 --> 00:15:04.680
And perhaps that was driven by poverty or lack

344
00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:07.040
or inaccessibility of other types of foods.

345
00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:08.370
But essentially,

346
00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:12.320
she was preparing predominantly vegetarian meals

347
00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:13.640
when you were a child, right?

348
00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:14.473
Absolutely.

349
00:15:14.473 --> 00:15:18.330
We had a beautiful garden in our backyard.

350
00:15:18.330 --> 00:15:21.250
Wherever we lived, you know, in whatever house we had,

351
00:15:21.250 --> 00:15:23.770
the first thing she did was she started planting.

352
00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:25.850
So we had, you know, sesame leaves.

353
00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:28.110
We had, you know, jalapeno peppers,

354
00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:32.030
we had tomatoes, zucchini, we had fruit trees.

355
00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:36.310
I mean, it was just this bounty always in our backyard.

356
00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:37.860
And it was somewhat driven

357
00:15:37.860 --> 00:15:41.190
by this kind of sense of security.

358
00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:44.760
You know, there's nothing more fundamentally secure and safe

359
00:15:44.760 --> 00:15:47.650
than being able to produce your own food.

360
00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:48.510
And that's what she did.

361
00:15:48.510 --> 00:15:51.270
She'd done that her whole life, she'd always been a farmer.

362
00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:55.210
And so yeah, our table was largely vegetables.

363
00:15:55.210 --> 00:15:58.920
Korea is a peninsula, so there's a lot of seafood involved.

364
00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:00.720
So sometimes we would have fish.

365
00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:02.660
But meat was very rare.

366
00:16:02.660 --> 00:16:03.898
It's like a special occasion kind of thing.

367
00:16:03.898 --> 00:16:04.740
Yeah.

368
00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:08.160
And yet when you go vegan,

369
00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:10.580
and you become The Korean Vegan,

370
00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:12.391
I can't imagine that went down so well

371
00:16:12.391 --> 00:16:16.290
(Joanne chuckles)
with the extended family.

372
00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:20.517
So my parents, you know, they just assumed,

373
00:16:20.517 --> 00:16:22.840
"Oh, there she goes again, she's dieting.

374
00:16:22.840 --> 00:16:24.360
She's just trying to lose weight."

375
00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:26.110
And yeah, I've been fluctuating

376
00:16:26.110 --> 00:16:27.750
with my weight my whole life.

377
00:16:27.750 --> 00:16:29.920
And so that's what they assumed.

378
00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:33.290
And when they realized, "Oh, okay, it's not just a diet."

379
00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:35.730
Then it was like, "Well, you're running all these marathons.

380
00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:37.840
You can't do that. You're just gonna die one day,

381
00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:39.680
if you don't eat animal protein.

382
00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:42.840
You gotta eat animal protein and milk and things like that."

383
00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:46.720
And so they weren't entirely sold on it.

384
00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:49.450
A lot of them probably still are not.

385
00:16:49.450 --> 00:16:51.408
But they're very polite, very respectful.

386
00:16:51.408 --> 00:16:52.241
Yeah.

387
00:16:52.241 --> 00:16:54.110
And my mom, I think she understands.

388
00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:57.490
But certainly is difficult for them.

389
00:16:57.490 --> 00:17:00.280
I think for the larger Korean-American community,

390
00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:01.490
I think there are probably a lot

391
00:17:01.490 --> 00:17:03.900
who view me as not very Korean

392
00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:06.020
or sort of a hack or whatever,

393
00:17:06.020 --> 00:17:08.150
you know, cultural appropriation, you name it.

394
00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:10.010
I've been accused of everything.

395
00:17:10.010 --> 00:17:10.843
Yeah.

396
00:17:10.843 --> 00:17:12.790
I mean, that response from your family

397
00:17:12.790 --> 00:17:15.620
feels relatively normal.

398
00:17:15.620 --> 00:17:17.290
I mean, a lot of people experience that.

399
00:17:17.290 --> 00:17:21.080
But what part of that comes from this idea

400
00:17:21.080 --> 00:17:24.700
that you're betraying your cultural heritage, you know?

401
00:17:24.700 --> 00:17:26.630
I don't think they think that at all.

402
00:17:26.630 --> 00:17:28.590
I don't think my family does.

403
00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:31.620
Because I mean, if anything,

404
00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:35.590
I'm so much more connected to my heritage than I ever was.

405
00:17:35.590 --> 00:17:37.580
Before I started The Korean Vegan,

406
00:17:37.580 --> 00:17:41.450
I ate Italian-American food, I ate Thai food.

407
00:17:41.450 --> 00:17:43.130
It was mostly like takeout,

408
00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:45.050
or whatever I could get at a restaurant.

409
00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:47.610
Now, I'm calling my mom every three days

410
00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:49.920
to be like, "Hey, how do you make (indistinct) again?

411
00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:53.080
Or how do I make my pajeon as crispy as yours?

412
00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:55.640
And I'm constantly asking her for advice

413
00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:59.420
on cooking the food that she thought I hated whole life.

414
00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:01.220
Yeah.
So it's the exact opposite.

415
00:18:01.220 --> 00:18:02.480
Right? It's interesting.

416
00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:04.570
This exploration of food and cuisine

417
00:18:04.570 --> 00:18:08.130
has really brought you closer to those roots.

418
00:18:08.130 --> 00:18:11.460
And it's been this journey, this exploration into your past

419
00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:13.360
that has wed you to it

420
00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:15.530
in a way that perhaps never would have happened

421
00:18:15.530 --> 00:18:17.200
had you not embarked on this.

422
00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:21.460
That's 100% true. And it's incredibly rewarding.

423
00:18:21.460 --> 00:18:25.370
And I'm very grateful that I've sort of taken this journey.

424
00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:28.160
Explain to me the cultural appropriation piece though,

425
00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:29.640
because that's confusing to me

426
00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:32.720
as somebody who, you know, is Korean.

427
00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:34.430
So, I'm Korean-American.

428
00:18:34.430 --> 00:18:37.770
So already, you know, I'm a step sort of away

429
00:18:37.770 --> 00:18:40.880
from authentic, if you wanna use that word,

430
00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:42.640
or traditional Korean food.

431
00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:44.600
And, you know, I just had this experience the other day,

432
00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:46.410
I made kimchi fried rice

433
00:18:46.410 --> 00:18:50.300
for my TikTok and my sister-in-law who's a native Korean,

434
00:18:50.300 --> 00:18:51.610
she was in the kitchen with me

435
00:18:51.610 --> 00:18:52.650
and I said, "Hey, try this."

436
00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:55.580
You know? And she's like, "Hmm, it's delicious,

437
00:18:55.580 --> 00:18:57.770
but it's not traditional Korean." (laughs)

438
00:18:57.770 --> 00:19:00.360
You know, this is Korean-American version.

439
00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:01.193
And I was like, "All right."

440
00:19:01.193 --> 00:19:03.610
But she said, you know, "It was very delicious."

441
00:19:03.610 --> 00:19:06.663
And I think, you know, in terms of cultural appropriation,

442
00:19:08.140 --> 00:19:11.840
there's this idea that I'm taking liberties

443
00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:15.500
and inappropriate liberties with Korean cuisine

444
00:19:15.500 --> 00:19:18.070
by making the adjustments that I need to

445
00:19:18.070 --> 00:19:20.470
in order to ensure that it's plant-based,

446
00:19:20.470 --> 00:19:22.430
whether it's cutting out the meat

447
00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:26.090
or, you know, replacing the meat with,

448
00:19:26.090 --> 00:19:29.130
you know, some type of meat alternative

449
00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:30.293
or something like that.

450
00:19:31.220 --> 00:19:33.230
I think there's a fear,

451
00:19:33.230 --> 00:19:35.680
and an understandable one to some degree,

452
00:19:35.680 --> 00:19:38.170
that I'm somehow whitewashing Korean food.

453
00:19:38.170 --> 00:19:40.240
Oh, I see. I see.

454
00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:42.690
So in doing that,

455
00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:45.817
you're not appropriating you're misappropriating, right?

456
00:19:45.817 --> 00:19:47.660
So, lawyer a lawyer. You get it.

457
00:19:47.660 --> 00:19:49.150
That's what I always say to people.

458
00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:51.520
Like appropriation can actually be okay.

459
00:19:51.520 --> 00:19:53.470
Misappropriation is never okay.

460
00:19:53.470 --> 00:19:55.220
At least to the people who accused me of it.

461
00:19:55.220 --> 00:19:58.270
You're denigrating the purity of the tradition

462
00:19:58.270 --> 00:20:01.604
by spinning it in an American, you know?

463
00:20:01.604 --> 00:20:02.750
Putting an American flare on it.

464
00:20:02.750 --> 00:20:03.769
In an American Vegan way. Yeah.

465
00:20:03.769 --> 00:20:05.269
Right. Right. Right.
Sometimes so-

466
00:20:05.269 --> 00:20:06.102
How dare you.

467
00:20:06.102 --> 00:20:08.473
Yeah. How dare I add kale to my (speaks foreign language)

468
00:20:08.473 --> 00:20:09.790
(Joanne laughs)
Yeah.

469
00:20:09.790 --> 00:20:10.960
That's so interesting.

470
00:20:10.960 --> 00:20:14.650
I mean, that begs the larger question of you now

471
00:20:14.650 --> 00:20:15.703
with like millions of people

472
00:20:15.703 --> 00:20:17.593
that are following what you're doing,

473
00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:21.110
on some level that exposes you

474
00:20:21.110 --> 00:20:24.690
to a higher volume of criticism, I would suspect.

475
00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:28.530
So how do you navigate the vulnerability

476
00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:30.930
that you demonstrate consistently

477
00:20:30.930 --> 00:20:33.460
against the trolls that are out there

478
00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:35.920
and people who, you know, are of mal-intent

479
00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:37.950
who are looking to, you know, take you down

480
00:20:37.950 --> 00:20:39.043
or destabilize you.

481
00:20:40.530 --> 00:20:44.670
It's a difficult process and I wasn't ready for it.

482
00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:46.370
I wasn't prepared for it.

483
00:20:46.370 --> 00:20:50.360
I had had my social media for four and a half years,

484
00:20:50.360 --> 00:20:52.560
and as, you know, growing steadily,

485
00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:57.040
but never like to a point where I felt the negative comments

486
00:20:57.040 --> 00:20:58.210
would really bother me.

487
00:20:58.210 --> 00:20:59.490
And they weren't really bothering me

488
00:20:59.490 --> 00:21:03.610
because the overwhelming reaction was always so positive.

489
00:21:03.610 --> 00:21:08.050
And then when my TikTok sort of exploded

490
00:21:08.050 --> 00:21:11.210
and my audience grew so exponentially

491
00:21:11.210 --> 00:21:13.163
in such a very short period of time,

492
00:21:14.050 --> 00:21:17.400
predictably, the negativity also went with it.

493
00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:19.030
It arose with it.

494
00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:21.710
And I was not emotionally prepared for it.

495
00:21:21.710 --> 00:21:25.640
Because as you know, my stories are very personal

496
00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:28.400
and, you know, people think, "Oh, it must be so easy for her

497
00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:29.820
'cause she does it all the time."

498
00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:32.830
And yes, I'm comfortable to a point,

499
00:21:32.830 --> 00:21:35.773
but my vulnerability is still very raw.

500
00:21:36.703 --> 00:21:41.703
And when people react so negatively and so personally

501
00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:47.680
to my very personal stories, it does hurt me.

502
00:21:47.950 --> 00:21:51.040
It hurts me very, I think, understandably,

503
00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:52.540
it hurts my feelings.

504
00:21:52.540 --> 00:21:55.830
And I'm also always trying to be so intentional

505
00:21:55.830 --> 00:21:59.937
with my content that I start to second guess myself.

506
00:21:59.937 --> 00:22:01.780
"Oh, did I say something

507
00:22:01.780 --> 00:22:05.040
that could be reasonably misinterpreted?"

508
00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:06.630
I mean, as a lawyer, again,

509
00:22:06.630 --> 00:22:08.690
I'm always trying to be very clear with my communication.

510
00:22:08.690 --> 00:22:11.270
I'm always wondering, did I say something wrong?

511
00:22:11.270 --> 00:22:15.180
Am I actually, you know, culturally appropriating spaghetti?

512
00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:17.950
Like, you know, could I have done better?

513
00:22:17.950 --> 00:22:19.740
And that sort of second guessing

514
00:22:19.740 --> 00:22:21.540
can be very agonizing for me

515
00:22:21.540 --> 00:22:23.300
because I'm trying to make sure

516
00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:27.230
that my communication is very clear, it's very positive,

517
00:22:27.230 --> 00:22:30.030
and that it isn't something that can be twisted

518
00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:31.180
into something negative.

519
00:22:31.180 --> 00:22:32.160
Yeah.

520
00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:34.540
It's interesting how the lawyer mind comes into play

521
00:22:34.540 --> 00:22:35.463
with all of that.

522
00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:39.650
It's a strength and a weakness, right?

523
00:22:39.650 --> 00:22:41.720
Because as a lawyer you're trained

524
00:22:41.720 --> 00:22:44.720
to objectively analyze arguments.

525
00:22:44.720 --> 00:22:46.300
So you can take an objective view

526
00:22:46.300 --> 00:22:48.710
of these variety of arguments.

527
00:22:48.710 --> 00:22:51.900
And for me, I always find myself in this gray area

528
00:22:51.900 --> 00:22:54.170
because I can get behind,

529
00:22:54.170 --> 00:22:56.850
you know, different sides of the equation,

530
00:22:56.850 --> 00:22:59.030
but that does lead to that second guessing,

531
00:22:59.030 --> 00:23:00.650
you're like, "I see that person's point,"

532
00:23:00.650 --> 00:23:04.220
rather than just being an advocate for yourself

533
00:23:04.220 --> 00:23:06.560
and kind of doubling down on your self-belief

534
00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:07.410
or your convictions.

535
00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:09.940
Actually, I think that's a great way of putting it.

536
00:23:09.940 --> 00:23:12.620
You know, how when you're supposed to write a memo,

537
00:23:12.620 --> 00:23:15.150
you know, the memo is supposed to be a completely objective,

538
00:23:15.150 --> 00:23:18.130
factual analysis, you know, legal analysis

539
00:23:18.130 --> 00:23:19.040
of a given question.

540
00:23:19.040 --> 00:23:20.560
You're not supposed to take a position.

541
00:23:20.560 --> 00:23:24.060
You're just supposed to apply the law to the set of facts.

542
00:23:24.060 --> 00:23:27.680
And my brain is hardwired to be that way.

543
00:23:27.680 --> 00:23:30.910
So I always am looking at both sides of things.

544
00:23:30.910 --> 00:23:33.590
And sometimes I feel like,

545
00:23:33.590 --> 00:23:36.640
especially, these days, people want you to take a position

546
00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:39.120
and I'm always like, "Let me just do the research

547
00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:42.340
and let me settle into things before I share my position."

548
00:23:42.340 --> 00:23:43.610
Well, my theory on that,

549
00:23:43.610 --> 00:23:45.900
because I think that's relatively unique

550
00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:47.320
in the legal profession.

551
00:23:47.320 --> 00:23:49.050
The lawyers that I used to work with

552
00:23:49.050 --> 00:23:50.269
didn't really have that problem.

553
00:23:50.269 --> 00:23:51.420
(Joanne laughs)

554
00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:54.330
And I think it has to do with your empathetic disposition.

555
00:23:54.330 --> 00:23:57.560
The more empathetic you are with that specific skillset,

556
00:23:57.560 --> 00:23:58.730
makes it very difficult.

557
00:23:58.730 --> 00:24:00.820
And I think my weakness as a lawyer

558
00:24:00.820 --> 00:24:02.760
was that I could always see the merit

559
00:24:04.144 --> 00:24:05.045
on the other side of the table.

560
00:24:05.045 --> 00:24:05.878
Mmm. Mm-hmm.

561
00:24:05.878 --> 00:24:07.387
And I would find myself thinking,

562
00:24:07.387 --> 00:24:08.948
"I wish I was the lawyer for the other side."

563
00:24:08.948 --> 00:24:11.013
(both laugh)
Like, I would have trouble.

564
00:24:11.013 --> 00:24:13.377
Like, whereas my colleagues would be like,

565
00:24:13.377 --> 00:24:16.140
"No, you're on this side and this is what we're doing."

566
00:24:16.140 --> 00:24:18.040
And I'd be like, "Yeah, I don't know, man.

567
00:24:18.040 --> 00:24:19.880
I don't think we have such a good case."

568
00:24:19.880 --> 00:24:22.020
We have terrible facts. (laughs)

569
00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:24.260
I know. Which makes it hard.

570
00:24:24.260 --> 00:24:27.070
Well, there was that one video that you made,

571
00:24:27.070 --> 00:24:29.760
there was a hater who said some racist bullshit.

572
00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:33.450
And you just decimated that person in 60 seconds

573
00:24:33.450 --> 00:24:36.900
with a battery of unassailable arguments.

574
00:24:36.900 --> 00:24:40.380
But I don't know if it was that video or another video,

575
00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:43.350
but your law firm told you, you had to take it down.

576
00:24:43.350 --> 00:24:44.710
This is an old,

577
00:24:44.710 --> 00:24:48.360
this is actually probably one of my first viral videos.

578
00:24:48.360 --> 00:24:50.640
And again, I was very unprepared.

579
00:24:50.640 --> 00:24:52.830
I didn't really even know what virality meant-

580
00:24:52.830 --> 00:24:55.020
Mm-hmm.
Particularly in TikTok.

581
00:24:55.020 --> 00:25:00.020
And it was a 60 second clap back as the kids call it.

582
00:25:00.130 --> 00:25:01.176
And I posted it.
Look at you

583
00:25:01.176 --> 00:25:02.200
with the lingo.
I know.

584
00:25:02.200 --> 00:25:04.640
I'm getting there. (laughs)

585
00:25:04.640 --> 00:25:08.400
But yeah, so I posted a fun sort of day in the life

586
00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:11.160
of a lawyer in Chicago during quarantine.

587
00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:12.650
And it was 60 second video.

588
00:25:12.650 --> 00:25:15.707
And it really was designed to show people,

589
00:25:15.707 --> 00:25:18.360
"Hey, I am a lawyer at a big law firm,

590
00:25:18.360 --> 00:25:19.820
but also have this other life.

591
00:25:19.820 --> 00:25:22.470
I run in the morning and I do food blogging at night.

592
00:25:22.470 --> 00:25:23.400
You can do it too."

593
00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:24.880
That was the whole idea.

594
00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:26.960
And of course, another lawyer,

595
00:25:26.960 --> 00:25:29.760
another woman lawyer responds to my,

596
00:25:29.760 --> 00:25:31.547
what I thought was a very cute video with,

597
00:25:31.547 --> 00:25:33.570
"It doesn't look like you work very hard."

598
00:25:33.570 --> 00:25:35.180
And that was the wrong comment to make at the time

599
00:25:35.180 --> 00:25:37.960
I think I was projected out at like 2,700 hours.

600
00:25:37.960 --> 00:25:38.960
I was working around the clock.

601
00:25:38.960 --> 00:25:40.450
I had a trial coming up.

602
00:25:40.450 --> 00:25:43.420
And I was so taken aback

603
00:25:43.420 --> 00:25:45.910
and I didn't really know how to react to it.

604
00:25:45.910 --> 00:25:47.500
But I was like, you know what,

605
00:25:47.500 --> 00:25:49.870
I'm gonna take this woman down like 60 seconds.

606
00:25:49.870 --> 00:25:50.830
That's what I got.

607
00:25:50.830 --> 00:25:53.350
I wrote down my rebuttal.

608
00:25:53.350 --> 00:25:56.210
And I basically just kind of spit it out.

609
00:25:56.210 --> 00:25:57.817
And, you know, I told her,

610
00:25:57.817 --> 00:26:00.160
"Look, I'm actually working very hard right now.

611
00:26:00.160 --> 00:26:03.610
And this is literally, how can you possibly tell

612
00:26:03.610 --> 00:26:06.630
how hard I work within a 60 second video?"

613
00:26:06.630 --> 00:26:10.710
And also, why are we so obsessed

614
00:26:10.710 --> 00:26:12.520
with working hard all the time?

615
00:26:12.520 --> 00:26:17.340
Why must I prove that to you as a good lawyer,

616
00:26:17.340 --> 00:26:19.620
that I somehow have to be what breaking my back,

617
00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:21.270
working all the time.

618
00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:23.450
And I did that and it went viral.

619
00:26:23.450 --> 00:26:26.560
And a lot of lawyer blogs picked it up.

620
00:26:26.560 --> 00:26:28.877
And I got a call from my CEO being like,

621
00:26:28.877 --> 00:26:31.650
"What is this TikTok thing? (laughs)

622
00:26:31.650 --> 00:26:33.110
Why are you doing this?"

623
00:26:33.110 --> 00:26:35.760
And I was like, "Don't worry, I've already deleted it."

624
00:26:35.760 --> 00:26:37.090
I was totally freaked out

625
00:26:37.090 --> 00:26:40.320
that I was gonna get fired or something. (laughs)

626
00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:42.330
But yeah, I ended up deleting that one.

627
00:26:42.330 --> 00:26:45.660
But they've since kind of gotten much more acclimated

628
00:26:45.660 --> 00:26:48.540
with the fact that I now have this presence on social media.

629
00:26:48.540 --> 00:26:53.080
Yeah. It has to be, I mean, it's a unique situation.

630
00:26:53.080 --> 00:26:55.963
But somewhat unsettling to the traditional legal community.

631
00:26:55.963 --> 00:26:57.870
I mean, this is a community of people

632
00:26:57.870 --> 00:26:59.580
that are by and large analog,

633
00:26:59.580 --> 00:27:01.760
like they don't have social media accounts.

634
00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:04.440
If they do, they're lurkers, they're not posting.

635
00:27:04.440 --> 00:27:08.140
It's very unusual for somebody in this profession

636
00:27:08.140 --> 00:27:11.057
to be public facing in that way.

637
00:27:11.057 --> 00:27:13.470
And my sense is that by and large,

638
00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:15.820
the legal community frowns upon that

639
00:27:15.820 --> 00:27:17.140
or is threatened by that.

640
00:27:17.140 --> 00:27:20.150
Because, you know, they're trained to look at things

641
00:27:20.150 --> 00:27:22.230
from a perspective of liabilities, right?

642
00:27:22.230 --> 00:27:25.650
So whether it's a client who sees you doing that,

643
00:27:25.650 --> 00:27:27.650
they want their clients to think that all you're doing

644
00:27:27.650 --> 00:27:29.460
is thinking about them all the time.

645
00:27:29.460 --> 00:27:31.450
And if you're making recipe videos,

646
00:27:31.450 --> 00:27:33.600
you're not working on their case

647
00:27:33.600 --> 00:27:36.917
and that's a difficult equation to square.

648
00:27:36.917 --> 00:27:37.750
It is.

649
00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:40.800
And I think I had all of those thoughts as well.

650
00:27:40.800 --> 00:27:44.460
When the legal blogs picked up the, you know, story

651
00:27:44.460 --> 00:27:47.750
that, you know, lawyer claps back and goes viral.

652
00:27:47.750 --> 00:27:50.050
I was so worried that my clients

653
00:27:50.050 --> 00:27:52.240
would start viewing me as unprofessional

654
00:27:52.240 --> 00:27:54.910
or not spending enough time on their cases

655
00:27:54.910 --> 00:27:56.880
or not being serious enough.

656
00:27:56.880 --> 00:27:59.590
But surprisingly, I actually received emails

657
00:27:59.590 --> 00:28:00.537
from some of my clients saying,

658
00:28:00.537 --> 00:28:03.820
"Hey, I saw that great piece about you and the clap back.

659
00:28:03.820 --> 00:28:05.190
I think you're amazing."

660
00:28:05.190 --> 00:28:07.510
And you know, many of my clients,

661
00:28:07.510 --> 00:28:09.050
you know, they follow me on Instagram,

662
00:28:09.050 --> 00:28:10.370
they follow me on Facebook,

663
00:28:10.370 --> 00:28:12.240
they're sharing my articles,

664
00:28:12.240 --> 00:28:14.520
they're sharing my posts to their friends.

665
00:28:14.520 --> 00:28:17.460
And so I think what we're seeing

666
00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:22.460
is a shift in the workforce in general,

667
00:28:22.860 --> 00:28:26.780
and that is applying to the legal industry as well.

668
00:28:26.780 --> 00:28:29.970
My clients understand I am not a robot.

669
00:28:29.970 --> 00:28:32.640
And that maybe they can actually have

670
00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:35.730
a more satisfying relationship with their attorney

671
00:28:35.730 --> 00:28:38.190
if they view them as human as well.

672
00:28:38.190 --> 00:28:39.950
At least that's been the experience for me.

673
00:28:39.950 --> 00:28:40.783
Yeah.

674
00:28:40.783 --> 00:28:42.240
That's very cool to hear.

675
00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:44.210
I've been very lucky. My clients are amazing.

676
00:28:44.210 --> 00:28:45.810
I have amazing clients.
Yeah.

677
00:28:46.870 --> 00:28:48.642
I mean, what are your fellow partners

678
00:28:48.642 --> 00:28:51.680
and the people in the law firm make of all of this?

679
00:28:51.680 --> 00:28:53.410
A lot of them don't know about it.

680
00:28:53.410 --> 00:28:54.951
How could they not know?
(Joanne laughs)

681
00:28:54.951 --> 00:28:58.430
I mean, you're on like national news and stuff like that.

682
00:28:58.430 --> 00:28:59.467
Listen, it's one thing to,

683
00:28:59.467 --> 00:29:01.730
"Oh, I have The Korean Vegan food blog."

684
00:29:01.730 --> 00:29:03.066
That's one thing.

685
00:29:03.066 --> 00:29:03.899
(Joanne laughs)
Phase one, all right?

686
00:29:03.899 --> 00:29:05.710
You do that for a couple of years.

687
00:29:05.710 --> 00:29:08.550
Then you're on Instagram and that gets kind of big.

688
00:29:08.550 --> 00:29:10.300
And then phase three,

689
00:29:10.300 --> 00:29:13.070
this TikTok thing is like an unbelievable phenomenon.

690
00:29:13.070 --> 00:29:15.230
I suppose, yeah, lawyers are not on TikTok

691
00:29:15.230 --> 00:29:16.063
so how would they know?

692
00:29:16.063 --> 00:29:18.710
But when that bleeds out into the culture

693
00:29:18.710 --> 00:29:21.690
and you're on, you know, X morning show or whatever,

694
00:29:21.690 --> 00:29:25.000
they're gonna get a glimpse of, you know?

695
00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:26.950
What's actually happening here?

696
00:29:26.950 --> 00:29:30.220
A lot of my closer partners have called me

697
00:29:30.220 --> 00:29:31.790
and they've congratulated me.

698
00:29:31.790 --> 00:29:33.880
I think there's a little bit of envy

699
00:29:33.880 --> 00:29:35.880
that lines some of our conversations,

700
00:29:35.880 --> 00:29:38.710
but mostly they're very supportive

701
00:29:38.710 --> 00:29:40.563
and they're very happy for me.

702
00:29:41.420 --> 00:29:44.010
And I think that the younger lawyers,

703
00:29:44.010 --> 00:29:45.260
you know, some of the associates

704
00:29:45.260 --> 00:29:46.680
or even some of the summer associates

705
00:29:46.680 --> 00:29:47.930
who've reached out to me,

706
00:29:49.380 --> 00:29:53.720
I always feel so lucky to have conversations with them

707
00:29:53.720 --> 00:29:56.890
because many of them have called me or emailed me

708
00:29:56.890 --> 00:30:01.600
and say, "I'm so inspired that you're a successful partner,

709
00:30:01.600 --> 00:30:04.067
but you also have this other thing that inspires you

710
00:30:04.067 --> 00:30:06.970
and that makes you feel whole."

711
00:30:06.970 --> 00:30:08.710
And that also makes me feel good.

712
00:30:08.710 --> 00:30:10.240
Because I didn't really have that

713
00:30:10.240 --> 00:30:11.420
when I was a junior associate,

714
00:30:11.420 --> 00:30:13.520
I was told, "Oh, expect to sleep on the floor

715
00:30:13.520 --> 00:30:14.527
a couple of nights a year."

716
00:30:14.527 --> 00:30:17.327
And you know, work 18 hours a day. And that's your life.

717
00:30:19.400 --> 00:30:20.960
That's a cultural shift.

718
00:30:20.960 --> 00:30:23.600
You know, when I was a young associate,

719
00:30:23.600 --> 00:30:25.720
you know, 10,000 years ago,

720
00:30:25.720 --> 00:30:27.920
I mean, it was just all about billable hours

721
00:30:27.920 --> 00:30:28.753
and that was it.

722
00:30:28.753 --> 00:30:29.586
And you're anonymous.

723
00:30:29.586 --> 00:30:32.240
And to kind of stand out in any way

724
00:30:32.240 --> 00:30:34.450
was considered, you know, anathema.

725
00:30:34.450 --> 00:30:37.890
Like you're a cog in a wheel and we need you to grind.

726
00:30:37.890 --> 00:30:40.030
And that's the end of it.

727
00:30:40.030 --> 00:30:41.380
It is dangerous.

728
00:30:41.380 --> 00:30:44.950
You know, there's certain level of risk of sticking out.

729
00:30:44.950 --> 00:30:49.220
I will say, and, you know, my firm is probably more humane

730
00:30:49.220 --> 00:30:50.880
than a lot of large law firms.

731
00:30:50.880 --> 00:30:52.303
It always has been.

732
00:30:53.560 --> 00:30:56.240
I really can't say enough good things about my firm,

733
00:30:56.240 --> 00:30:58.750
just because they've been very supportive of me,

734
00:30:58.750 --> 00:31:00.530
even as a summer intern.

735
00:31:00.530 --> 00:31:02.807
You know, they were always like,

736
00:31:02.807 --> 00:31:05.710
"You're a rockstar and we expect amazing things from you."

737
00:31:05.710 --> 00:31:07.070
And they did everything they could

738
00:31:07.070 --> 00:31:09.147
to make sure I catapulted to success.

739
00:31:09.147 --> 00:31:11.120
Right.
And I went through

740
00:31:11.120 --> 00:31:12.130
a lot of personal issues.

741
00:31:12.130 --> 00:31:15.160
I went through a divorce, it was difficult, it was horrible.

742
00:31:15.160 --> 00:31:17.690
And they were with me every step of the way.

743
00:31:17.690 --> 00:31:20.590
So, you know, I think like people,

744
00:31:20.590 --> 00:31:23.260
law firms can have a range of personalities

745
00:31:23.260 --> 00:31:25.463
and I lucked out and I picked a good one.

746
00:31:26.430 --> 00:31:27.840
Sorry to interrupt the flow.

747
00:31:27.840 --> 00:31:29.500
We'll be right back with more awesome.

748
00:31:29.500 --> 00:31:31.810
But I want a snag a moment to talk to you

749
00:31:31.810 --> 00:31:34.630
about the importance of nutrition.

750
00:31:34.630 --> 00:31:36.870
The thing is, most people I know

751
00:31:36.870 --> 00:31:39.150
actually already know how to eat better

752
00:31:39.150 --> 00:31:42.520
and aspire to incorporate more whole plants,

753
00:31:42.520 --> 00:31:44.700
more fruits, vegetables, seeds, beans,

754
