00:34:55.050 --> 00:34:58.720
He's the one who can afford to, you know, be creative.

834
00:34:58.720 --> 00:35:00.430
And I'm like, "Okay, I wanna be creative too,

835
00:35:00.430 --> 00:35:05.430
but I have deadlines, I have productivity requirements.

836
00:35:06.180 --> 00:35:08.210
And you know, I have clients who are calling me,

837
00:35:08.210 --> 00:35:09.340
setting up meetings."

838
00:35:09.340 --> 00:35:13.660
And so timing, I think, is one aspect of it.

839
00:35:13.660 --> 00:35:16.180
For me, the challenge is much more

840
00:35:16.180 --> 00:35:18.900
about the different areas of my brain

841
00:35:18.900 --> 00:35:22.540
that are required to be on point at a given time.

842
00:35:22.540 --> 00:35:24.730
You know, and COVID has certainly,

843
00:35:24.730 --> 00:35:26.830
you know, the silver lining of COVID I guess

844
00:35:26.830 --> 00:35:28.230
is I've been at home.

845
00:35:28.230 --> 00:35:32.260
So my schedule is in fact, a lot more flexible

846
00:35:32.260 --> 00:35:33.410
than it used to be.

847
00:35:33.410 --> 00:35:37.280
If I have a 15 minute break between phone calls,

848
00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:40.400
I can actually go to my, you know, photo editing computer

849
00:35:40.400 --> 00:35:42.270
and maybe edit a couple of things

850
00:35:42.270 --> 00:35:44.270
or write down a couple of things.

851
00:35:44.270 --> 00:35:46.210
I used to think that that would work.

852
00:35:46.210 --> 00:35:48.270
And in the beginning, I think it did.

853
00:35:48.270 --> 00:35:51.287
Perhaps because I was so excited by TikTok

854
00:35:51.287 --> 00:35:53.120
and the energy of it.

855
00:35:53.120 --> 00:35:55.460
But by now I have realized,

856
00:35:55.460 --> 00:35:58.750
the amount of energy required to transition

857
00:35:58.750 --> 00:36:03.640
from lawyer mode to Korean Vegan mode, it is not easy.

858
00:36:03.640 --> 00:36:06.450
I mean, there's a natural resistance.

859
00:36:06.450 --> 00:36:09.000
When I'm in the zone, you know, talking to clients,

860
00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:10.820
emailing, writing a brief,

861
00:36:10.820 --> 00:36:13.997
my brain doesn't wanna go to, "Let's edit this video now."

862
00:36:14.860 --> 00:36:16.840
But if you're editing a photo,

863
00:36:16.840 --> 00:36:20.200
and you're thinking about a client or a case,

864
00:36:20.200 --> 00:36:21.348
can you bill for that?

865
00:36:21.348 --> 00:36:22.181
No.

866
00:36:22.181 --> 00:36:23.070
(Rich laughs)

867
00:36:23.070 --> 00:36:25.589
No, no, no, no. We should do a CLE on ethics right now.

868
00:36:25.589 --> 00:36:27.255
I love that. The argument.

869
00:36:27.255 --> 00:36:28.672
Yeah. No, I know.

870
00:36:30.020 --> 00:36:32.900
You're very much in a certain respect,

871
00:36:32.900 --> 00:36:36.480
your father's daughter in that, like this very Type A person

872
00:36:36.480 --> 00:36:39.810
you grew up with a very emotionally reserved,

873
00:36:39.810 --> 00:36:43.343
I think it's fair to say, taciturn taskmaster.

874
00:36:44.400 --> 00:36:48.403
My dad is, he is taciturn.

875
00:36:50.490 --> 00:36:53.640
I would say the taskmaster is probably more my mother,

876
00:36:53.640 --> 00:36:57.890
but the discipline is certainly my father.

877
00:36:57.890 --> 00:37:02.890
My father has sort of extraordinary levels of discipline

878
00:37:03.710 --> 00:37:07.690
and he demands that of the people around him.

879
00:37:07.690 --> 00:37:09.680
I don't think he does it in any way

880
00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:11.443
like, "Oh, I demand more from you."

881
00:37:11.443 --> 00:37:13.970
It's just that is his normalcy.

882
00:37:13.970 --> 00:37:17.260
Is working in very structured environments

883
00:37:17.260 --> 00:37:20.430
and demanding absolute excellence.

884
00:37:20.430 --> 00:37:21.990
So when I was growing up,

885
00:37:21.990 --> 00:37:25.000
this is very typical Korean-American story.

886
00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:27.970
If I had it a report card that had a B plus,

887
00:37:27.970 --> 00:37:28.860
it was never good,

888
00:37:28.860 --> 00:37:31.157
an A minus even wasn't good enough for my dad.

889
00:37:31.157 --> 00:37:33.010
"Why don't you get 100?

890
00:37:33.010 --> 00:37:34.680
Why don't you have straight A pluses

891
00:37:34.680 --> 00:37:36.960
on all of your report cards?"

892
00:37:36.960 --> 00:37:41.570
And, you know, he was also the one every Sunday morning,

893
00:37:41.570 --> 00:37:43.460
which was the morning that he had off.

894
00:37:43.460 --> 00:37:46.110
You know, he'd bring out a stack of geometry books.

895
00:37:46.110 --> 00:37:48.192
And he said, "Joanne it's tutoring time."

896
00:37:48.192 --> 00:37:49.025
Oh my God.

897
00:37:49.025 --> 00:37:50.140
And, you know, three hours of math tutoring

898
00:37:50.140 --> 00:37:51.630
on my Sunday morning.

899
00:37:51.630 --> 00:37:54.220
And that was like, non-negotiable for my dad.

900
00:37:54.220 --> 00:37:55.900
It was either that or go play tennis.

901
00:37:55.900 --> 00:37:58.410
I mean, he's absolutely insane about tennis.

902
00:37:58.410 --> 00:38:00.840
And so, you know, "It's time for exercise,"

903
00:38:00.840 --> 00:38:02.280
so we'd have to go do that.

904
00:38:02.280 --> 00:38:04.840
So my dad is very disciplined.

905
00:38:04.840 --> 00:38:08.650
But again, I don't know if it's born out of a need

906
00:38:08.650 --> 00:38:12.770
to elicit excellent so much as it it's comforting to him

907
00:38:12.770 --> 00:38:14.770
to work within a structured environment.

908
00:38:14.770 --> 00:38:18.610
But that impulse, that very traditional Korean mindset

909
00:38:18.610 --> 00:38:20.470
and sensibility,

910
00:38:20.470 --> 00:38:21.500
where does that come from?

911
00:38:21.500 --> 00:38:24.540
Like how much of that is implicitly Korean

912
00:38:24.540 --> 00:38:29.540
versus the experience of the Korean immigrant

913
00:38:29.620 --> 00:38:33.690
trying to make their way in a very difficult America?

914
00:38:33.690 --> 00:38:36.220
I think that there are two pieces to it.

915
00:38:36.220 --> 00:38:38.110
That's a really good question actually.

916
00:38:38.110 --> 00:38:39.960
I certainly believe that

917
00:38:40.946 --> 00:38:45.030
the experience of the Korean diaspora weighs into

918
00:38:45.030 --> 00:38:49.030
that sort of expectation and that anxiety, I think,

919
00:38:49.030 --> 00:38:50.970
that really drives that sort of compulsion

920
00:38:50.970 --> 00:38:52.710
of everything needs to be in its box.

921
00:38:52.710 --> 00:38:55.040
You know, you always need to be at your best.

922
00:38:55.040 --> 00:38:56.350
You need to work the hardest,

923
00:38:56.350 --> 00:38:58.930
your work ethic has to be, you know, sky high.

924
00:38:58.930 --> 00:39:00.530
I certainly believe that.

925
00:39:00.530 --> 00:39:03.360
But I also think that again, as you mentioned,

926
00:39:03.360 --> 00:39:08.310
because of these multiple generations of war and poverty,

927
00:39:08.310 --> 00:39:11.580
and really just this instinct for survival

928
00:39:11.580 --> 00:39:13.330
that sort of kicks in,

929
00:39:13.330 --> 00:39:17.870
I think that anxiety also, you know,

930
00:39:17.870 --> 00:39:19.650
is always kind of underlying

931
00:39:19.650 --> 00:39:24.120
all of these, you know, restrictions on my activities.

932
00:39:24.120 --> 00:39:25.480
You know, I wasn't allowed to do theater.

933
00:39:25.480 --> 00:39:27.490
I had to do math club. (chuckles)

934
00:39:27.490 --> 00:39:30.320
You know, I couldn't sing, I had to play piano.

935
00:39:30.320 --> 00:39:33.300
Like all of these things sort of kind of go into that.

936
00:39:33.300 --> 00:39:34.133
Wow.

937
00:39:35.030 --> 00:39:35.983
Had to be hard.

938
00:39:36.990 --> 00:39:37.870
You know, what?

939
00:39:37.870 --> 00:39:40.950
It could be a little bit stifling at times,

940
00:39:40.950 --> 00:39:43.070
but, you know, in retrospect,

941
00:39:43.070 --> 00:39:46.650
my brother and I turned out to be really good kids

942
00:39:46.650 --> 00:39:48.110
and we were very happy.

943
00:39:48.110 --> 00:39:49.730
And at the end of the day,

944
00:39:49.730 --> 00:39:52.210
probably a lot to do with my grandmothers living with us

945
00:39:52.210 --> 00:39:55.830
and have having such a large piece of our raising.

946
00:39:55.830 --> 00:39:57.040
We were so safe.

947
00:39:57.040 --> 00:39:57.873
We were happy.

948
00:39:57.873 --> 00:40:00.160
We were safe and we had everything we could have wanted.

949
00:40:00.160 --> 00:40:01.990
We were loved immensely.

950
00:40:01.990 --> 00:40:04.760
And you know, my parents are amazing.

951
00:40:04.760 --> 00:40:08.470
I'm so grateful that I have such lovely parents.

952
00:40:08.470 --> 00:40:13.470
Not only are they Korean, they're both from North Korea.

953
00:40:13.890 --> 00:40:15.650
A fact that you were not made aware of

954
00:40:15.650 --> 00:40:19.040
until you were in college, which is baffling to me.

955
00:40:19.040 --> 00:40:20.100
To me too.
Talk a little bit

956
00:40:20.100 --> 00:40:23.860
about that, because that story or the multiple stories

957
00:40:23.860 --> 00:40:26.320
around your parents getting out of North Korea

958
00:40:26.320 --> 00:40:27.700
is pretty amazing.

959
00:40:27.700 --> 00:40:31.600
Yeah, so I think it was,

960
00:40:31.600 --> 00:40:33.420
I don't know if it was North Korea

961
00:40:33.420 --> 00:40:35.010
at the time my mother was born,

962
00:40:35.010 --> 00:40:37.100
certainly at the time my father was born,

963
00:40:37.100 --> 00:40:39.970
it wasn't North Korea, it was still sort of that gray area,

964
00:40:39.970 --> 00:40:41.170
like, "Okay, what's happening

965
00:40:41.170 --> 00:40:44.550
between the two pieces of the peninsula?"

966
00:40:44.550 --> 00:40:47.000
But yes, they were born in what is now

967
00:40:47.000 --> 00:40:49.170
certainly known as North Korea.

968
00:40:49.170 --> 00:40:52.190
And you're right, I had no idea until college.

969
00:40:52.190 --> 00:40:54.590
In fact, I was just looking at my mother's birth certificate

970
00:40:54.590 --> 00:40:56.490
the other day and it still says South Korea.

971
00:40:56.490 --> 00:40:58.810
And I'm like, "Oh, Oma, what is this?"

972
00:40:58.810 --> 00:40:59.963
And she just laughs.

973
00:41:00.870 --> 00:41:05.730
But yeah, I think some of it was because as kids,

974
00:41:05.730 --> 00:41:08.200
we just kind of took our parents' stories for granted.

975
00:41:08.200 --> 00:41:09.033
I didn't care.

976
00:41:09.033 --> 00:41:10.360
I was like, "Well, they're from South Korea.

977
00:41:10.360 --> 00:41:12.300
'Cause I go back to South Korea with them all the time.

978
00:41:12.300 --> 00:41:13.750
That must be their home."

979
00:41:13.750 --> 00:41:15.910
I never thought to ask,

980
00:41:15.910 --> 00:41:18.890
well, could they have been from that region,

981
00:41:18.890 --> 00:41:20.580
which is completely typical.

982
00:41:20.580 --> 00:41:23.020
Half the country is probably,

983
00:41:23.020 --> 00:41:26.220
you know, half South Korea is probably from North Korea.

984
00:41:26.220 --> 00:41:29.830
And then, you know, with a little bit of probing,

985
00:41:29.830 --> 00:41:33.110
you know, my mom would tell me these stories about escaping

986
00:41:33.110 --> 00:41:36.137
and like, "Where are you escaping from?"

987
00:41:36.137 --> 00:41:38.727
"Oh, well, you know, I'm North Korean and didn't you know?"

988
00:41:38.727 --> 00:41:41.060
"No, I didn't, that's an important fact."

989
00:41:41.060 --> 00:41:42.231
That's an interesting.

990
00:41:42.231 --> 00:41:43.064
Yeah.

991
00:41:43.064 --> 00:41:43.897
(both laugh)

992
00:41:43.897 --> 00:41:44.950
I did not know that.

993
00:41:44.950 --> 00:41:47.260
That's very interesting to me.
Oh my God.

994
00:41:47.260 --> 00:41:50.750
And my father similarly has a lot of stories

995
00:41:50.750 --> 00:41:52.090
from that time in his life.

996
00:41:52.090 --> 00:41:54.190
Particularly about his own father

997
00:41:54.190 --> 00:41:58.000
and his role during that conflict and the like.

998
00:41:58.000 --> 00:42:02.530
And you know, and I soon learned, "Oh, you're also from

999
00:42:02.530 --> 00:42:04.350
what is now known as North Korea.

1000
00:42:04.350 --> 00:42:05.840
I had no idea."

1001
00:42:05.840 --> 00:42:08.890
And unfortunately at that time, you know, in college,

1002
00:42:08.890 --> 00:42:13.270
I wasn't really educated on kind of what that meant.

1003
00:42:13.270 --> 00:42:15.440
All I knew was what I was hearing on the news.

1004
00:42:15.440 --> 00:42:17.360
Oh, North Korea is the axis of evil.

1005
00:42:17.360 --> 00:42:21.020
You know, North Korea is the butt of all these jokes

1006
00:42:21.020 --> 00:42:23.580
and it's a bad thing to be from North Korea.

1007
00:42:23.580 --> 00:42:26.960
So I was immediately embarrassed to discover my heritage,

1008
00:42:26.960 --> 00:42:30.040
which is now embarrassing to me.

1009
00:42:30.040 --> 00:42:35.040
The story of your mom immigrating south

1010
00:42:35.080 --> 00:42:37.500
on the back of your grandmother.

1011
00:42:37.500 --> 00:42:38.680
Can you tell that story?

1012
00:42:38.680 --> 00:42:39.513
Yeah.

1013
00:42:39.513 --> 00:42:42.520
So that is probably one of the earliest stories

1014
00:42:42.520 --> 00:42:44.740
my mom ever shared with me.

1015
00:42:44.740 --> 00:42:48.050
And we were eating like these sweet potatoes.

1016
00:42:48.050 --> 00:42:50.040
She eats sweet potatoes every day.

1017
00:42:50.040 --> 00:42:51.800
And she just like microwaves them

1018
00:42:51.800 --> 00:42:55.010
or sticks them in the microwave oven and eats them.

1019
00:42:55.010 --> 00:42:56.427
And she's like, "This is my favorite food."

1020
00:42:56.427 --> 00:42:58.767
I'm like, "Why is this your favorite food?"

1021
00:42:58.767 --> 00:43:00.500
And she was like, "Well, when we were refugees,

1022
00:43:00.500 --> 00:43:01.810
this is what we would eat."

1023
00:43:01.810 --> 00:43:04.220
She said she would dig through the soil,

1024
00:43:04.220 --> 00:43:06.260
you know, when everybody else was asleep

1025
00:43:06.260 --> 00:43:09.410
and she would look for whatever rotten sweet potatoes

1026
00:43:09.410 --> 00:43:10.650
were left in the soil.

1027
00:43:10.650 --> 00:43:13.300
I was like, "Refugees? Why were you refugees?

1028
00:43:13.300 --> 00:43:15.022
Where were you refugees from?"
Burying the lead.

1029
00:43:15.022 --> 00:43:16.360
(Joanne laughs)

1030
00:43:16.360 --> 00:43:17.212
What is that about?

1031
00:43:17.212 --> 00:43:18.670
In the context of the story about sweet potatoes.

1032
00:43:18.670 --> 00:43:19.780
Exactly.

1033
00:43:19.780 --> 00:43:22.040
And she was like, "Oh, well, you know,

1034
00:43:22.040 --> 00:43:24.160
I was born in North Korea."

1035
00:43:24.160 --> 00:43:25.950
Oh, okay.

1036
00:43:25.950 --> 00:43:29.660
Tell me about this, you know, refugee situation.

1037
00:43:29.660 --> 00:43:31.603
And she said, "Oh, I love this story.

1038
00:43:31.603 --> 00:43:33.380
This is my favorite story."

1039
00:43:33.380 --> 00:43:35.090
She said, "Your grandma almost killed me."

1040
00:43:35.090 --> 00:43:36.240
And I was like, "What?"

1041
00:43:38.019 --> 00:43:39.487
And I was like, "What are you talking about?"

1042
00:43:39.487 --> 00:43:42.869
"Oh yeah. The time your grandma almost murdered me."

1043
00:43:42.869 --> 00:43:44.010
And I was like, "Okay."

1044
00:43:44.010 --> 00:43:45.363
Saying it as if you already knew the story.

1045
00:43:45.363 --> 00:43:46.300
Yeah.

1046
00:43:46.300 --> 00:43:48.120
We've been talking about this the whole time.

1047
00:43:48.120 --> 00:43:51.120
Oh, this is, I think, typical of parents, right? All over.

1048
00:43:52.280 --> 00:43:55.500
And so she then explained to me

1049
00:43:55.500 --> 00:43:59.090
that when they were escaping North Korea

1050
00:43:59.090 --> 00:44:01.280
during the Korean War,

1051
00:44:01.280 --> 00:44:04.690
they only had what they could take with them.

1052
00:44:04.690 --> 00:44:06.590
This is like what you see in the movies.

1053
00:44:06.590 --> 00:44:08.380
They grabbed whatever they could grab.

1054
00:44:08.380 --> 00:44:09.360
And they had two children,

1055
00:44:09.360 --> 00:44:11.760
my mom and her older sister at the time.

1056
00:44:11.760 --> 00:44:14.530
And they had to walk to the Yellow Sea

1057
00:44:14.530 --> 00:44:16.510
because they were told that there was a boat

1058
00:44:16.510 --> 00:44:18.770
that would take them to safety.

1059
00:44:18.770 --> 00:44:22.630
And in my mind, I'm literally seeing all these old movies,

1060
00:44:22.630 --> 00:44:24.690
you know, where you see war torn countries

1061
00:44:24.690 --> 00:44:26.640
and people with, you know, sacks of food

1062
00:44:26.640 --> 00:44:27.473
and a little bit of water

1063
00:44:27.473 --> 00:44:29.610
and their babies, you know, swaddled to them.

1064
00:44:29.610 --> 00:44:32.060
And they're walking endless amounts

1065
00:44:32.060 --> 00:44:33.770
and they finally get somewhere.

1066
00:44:33.770 --> 00:44:35.940
And they get to this boat.

1067
00:44:35.940 --> 00:44:37.070
And by that time,

1068
00:44:37.070 --> 00:44:39.750
my mother who was, I think one and a half year old,

1069
00:44:39.750 --> 00:44:41.110
she was starving to death.

1070
00:44:41.110 --> 00:44:42.460
They had no food.

1071
00:44:42.460 --> 00:44:43.440
They had no water.

1072
00:44:43.440 --> 00:44:46.480
And I subsequently learned that it took them two weeks

1073
00:44:46.480 --> 00:44:48.040
to get to the Yellow Sea.

1074
00:44:48.040 --> 00:44:48.920
So if you can imagine

1075
00:44:48.920 --> 00:44:51.090
they had run out of the food and water,

1076
00:44:51.090 --> 00:44:52.510
you know, long before.

1077
00:44:52.510 --> 00:44:54.310
And my mom was screaming.

1078
00:44:54.310 --> 00:44:57.500
Apparently just screaming and screaming and screaming.

1079
00:44:57.500 --> 00:45:00.110
And you know, you have children.

1080
00:45:00.110 --> 00:45:01.410
I can't even imagine

1081
00:45:01.410 --> 00:45:04.440
what my grandparents must've been thinking and feeling

1082
00:45:04.440 --> 00:45:08.700
watching their baby girl, literally dying before their eyes.

1083
00:45:08.700 --> 00:45:12.000
And they had no idea when they were going to get food

1084
00:45:12.000 --> 00:45:15.590
or water again, if they were ever going to get to safety.

1085
00:45:15.590 --> 00:45:17.470
So they get on this ship.

1086
00:45:17.470 --> 00:45:22.470
My mom is screaming and crying for any food, any water.

1087
00:45:22.600 --> 00:45:27.237
And my grandfather sort of looks to my grandmother and says,

1088
00:45:27.237 --> 00:45:29.820
"I think we have to do something about Sonny."

1089
00:45:29.820 --> 00:45:31.610
That's what my mom calls herself.

1090
00:45:31.610 --> 00:45:34.240
Her Korean name is (indistinct) right?

1091
00:45:34.240 --> 00:45:35.407
And my grandmother was like,

1092
00:45:35.407 --> 00:45:36.880
"No, I don't know what you're thinking

1093
00:45:36.880 --> 00:45:38.600
or what you're suggesting, but no.

1094
00:45:38.600 --> 00:45:40.280
Like put it out of your head."

1095
00:45:40.280 --> 00:45:42.137
But my grandfather kept pleading with her.

1096
00:45:42.137 --> 00:45:45.280
"Look, our baby daughter is an agony.

1097
00:45:45.280 --> 00:45:46.940
We have to do something.

1098
00:45:46.940 --> 00:45:50.360
We can't just let her scream to death."

1099
00:45:50.360 --> 00:45:52.660
And ultimately what he was saying was,

1100
00:45:52.660 --> 00:45:54.770
we need to throw her in the water,

1101
00:45:54.770 --> 00:45:57.120
throw her into the sea and drown her.

1102
00:45:57.120 --> 00:45:59.660
And my grandmother at first was like, "Nope, not happening,

1103
00:45:59.660 --> 00:46:00.890
not my daughter."

1104
00:46:00.890 --> 00:46:03.160
But eventually, you know, again,

1105
00:46:03.160 --> 00:46:06.500
just watching her daughter scream and cry.

1106
00:46:06.500 --> 00:46:08.210
She ultimately decided, "Okay,

1107
00:46:08.210 --> 00:46:10.040
maybe that is the right decision."

1108
00:46:10.040 --> 00:46:13.410
And I'm sure many, many, many parents during that time

1109
00:46:13.410 --> 00:46:15.980
were grappling with the same sort of dilemma.

1110
00:46:15.980 --> 00:46:18.890
So they decided to go to the uppermost deck of the ship,

1111
00:46:18.890 --> 00:46:20.510
where there were not a lot of people.

1112
00:46:20.510 --> 00:46:24.100
And their plan was to essentially slip my mother overboard

1113
00:46:24.100 --> 00:46:26.490
and hopefully she would drown.

1114
00:46:26.490 --> 00:46:29.050
And so they're kind of at the edge of the ship,

1115
00:46:29.050 --> 00:46:30.060
like on the railing

1116
00:46:30.060 --> 00:46:33.310
and you know, everyone's crying at that point, right?

1117
00:46:33.310 --> 00:46:37.000
And apparently, a couple of American GIs

1118
00:46:37.000 --> 00:46:39.740
who were also on the ship, because it was a U.S. Naval ship.

1119
00:46:39.740 --> 00:46:43.550
They sort of saw, "Okay, what's going on over there?"

1120
00:46:43.550 --> 00:46:46.100
You know, family crying, their eyeballs out

1121
00:46:46.100 --> 00:46:49.160
and they're, you know, close to the edge of the ship there.

1122
00:46:49.160 --> 00:46:51.240
So they approached my grandparents

1123
00:46:51.240 --> 00:46:53.520
and of course my grandparents didn't speak any English,

1124
00:46:53.520 --> 00:46:55.400
but you know, they were trying to understand

1125
00:46:55.400 --> 00:46:56.233
what's going on.

1126
00:46:56.233 --> 00:46:58.240
Can we help you? What's happening?

1127
00:46:58.240 --> 00:47:01.780
And ultimately they realized what was happening was that

1128
00:47:01.780 --> 00:47:04.390
my mother was starving and she needed food.

1129
00:47:04.390 --> 00:47:06.380
And so he reached into his pocket

1130
00:47:06.380 --> 00:47:08.200
and pulled out a chocolate bar

1131
00:47:08.200 --> 00:47:10.070
and put it into my mom's hand.

1132
00:47:10.070 --> 00:47:12.870
And my mom says that is what saved her life.

1133
00:47:12.870 --> 00:47:14.400
Mm-hmm.

1134
00:47:14.400 --> 00:47:15.910
Such an incredible story.

1135
00:47:15.910 --> 00:47:16.743
It is.

1136
00:47:16.743 --> 00:47:20.622
It's a very heartwarming, wonderful sort of scary story.

1137
00:47:20.622 --> 00:47:21.455
Yeah.

1138
00:47:21.455 --> 00:47:24.240
But I'm thinking with GIs on that ship,

1139
00:47:24.240 --> 00:47:25.600
there was no food though.

1140
00:47:25.600 --> 00:47:28.627
Yeah, so there are a lot of holes when I asked my mom,

1141
00:47:28.627 --> 00:47:30.820
"I'm like, so what about your older sister?

1142
00:47:30.820 --> 00:47:32.930
Like, what was she doing during this time?"

1143
00:47:32.930 --> 00:47:33.763
Or, you know,

1144
00:47:33.763 --> 00:47:35.600
how could you think that there would be no food?

1145
00:47:35.600 --> 00:47:37.373
And, you know, I think,

1146
00:47:38.288 --> 00:47:40.530
obviously she was one and a half year old at the time too.

1147
00:47:40.530 --> 00:47:42.510
So it's not like she remembers any of this.

1148
00:47:42.510 --> 00:47:46.200
This is all kind of told to her from her parents.

1149
00:47:46.200 --> 00:47:47.910
But I think the idea was,

1150
00:47:47.910 --> 00:47:50.530
look, it wasn't like there was a buffet table

1151
00:47:50.530 --> 00:47:53.210
that, you know, "Hey everyone, refugees,

1152
00:47:53.210 --> 00:47:56.830
here's a whole mess of food for you to eat."

1153
00:47:56.830 --> 00:48:00.040
I don't think they had any idea what was happening.

1154
00:48:00.040 --> 00:48:02.010
They were sort of shuttled on board.

1155
00:48:02.010 --> 00:48:03.990
They didn't really know where it was gonna end up.

1156
00:48:03.990 --> 00:48:06.330
They had no idea whether there would be food

1157
00:48:06.330 --> 00:48:08.010
waiting for them or not.

1158
00:48:08.010 --> 00:48:09.140
You know, they didn't know

1159
00:48:09.140 --> 00:48:11.260
whether they were gonna end up in South Korea or not.

1160
00:48:11.260 --> 00:48:12.540
And they couldn't communicate.

1161
00:48:12.540 --> 00:48:14.140
Exactly language barriers

1162
00:48:14.140 --> 00:48:15.950
can be deadly in some situations.

1163
00:48:15.950 --> 00:48:17.790
And it nearly was in this case.

1164
00:48:17.790 --> 00:48:21.313
And I mean, thank goodness that they caught them in time

1165
00:48:21.313 --> 00:48:24.060
because they were literally about to slip her overboard

1166
00:48:24.060 --> 00:48:24.893
from what I understand.

1167
00:48:24.893 --> 00:48:27.290
Wow! It's such a crazy story.

1168
00:48:27.290 --> 00:48:30.320
And your dad's version of that story

1169
00:48:30.320 --> 00:48:33.429
is, you know, equally amazing.

1170
00:48:33.429 --> 00:48:34.262
With his parents and all that.
It is very

1171
00:48:34.262 --> 00:48:36.610
compelling.
Like, it's just crazy.

1172
00:48:36.610 --> 00:48:37.443
It is.

1173
00:48:37.443 --> 00:48:40.610
And again, it was told over food.

1174
00:48:40.610 --> 00:48:43.590
And it was like one of those like random situations,

1175
00:48:43.590 --> 00:48:46.750
my husband and I were eating at this restaurant.

1176
00:48:46.750 --> 00:48:47.950
I had invited my dad.

1177
00:48:47.950 --> 00:48:49.970
It was vegan japchae.

1178
00:48:49.970 --> 00:48:51.940
He's sitting there like cleaning his plate

1179
00:48:51.940 --> 00:48:53.320
and we're just like eating.

1180
00:48:53.320 --> 00:48:55.720
And he's like, "You know, about that time,

1181
00:48:55.720 --> 00:48:59.149
you know, grandma saved my life through swamp water?"

1182
00:48:59.149 --> 00:49:00.749
(Rich laughs)
I'm like, "What?"

1183
00:49:01.770 --> 00:49:05.050
And my husband and I are like, "What are you talking about?

1184
00:49:05.050 --> 00:49:06.427
Swamp water?"

1185
00:49:06.427 --> 00:49:07.590
"You know, yeah.

1186
00:49:07.590 --> 00:49:10.500
Your grandma saved life with my swamp water."

1187
00:49:10.500 --> 00:49:12.630
And we're like, "Okay, daddy.

1188
00:49:12.630 --> 00:49:15.200
Why don't you just tell us what you're talking about?"

1189
00:49:15.200 --> 00:49:20.200
And yeah, it was again, a situation where my grandmother,

1190
00:49:20.260 --> 00:49:22.330
she was acting as a single mom at the time

1191
00:49:22.330 --> 00:49:25.800
because my grandfather had already fled to South Korea

1192
00:49:25.800 --> 00:49:28.150
'cause he had worked for the Japanese police force

1193
00:49:28.150 --> 00:49:31.080
and was there for, you know, had a target on his back.

1194
00:49:31.080 --> 00:49:32.090
Right. They gotta get out.

1195
00:49:32.090 --> 00:49:35.290
He needed to get out. And ultimately she did as well.

1196
00:49:35.290 --> 00:49:37.480
And you know, there were communist soldiers

1197
00:49:37.480 --> 00:49:39.300
kind of guarding the 38th Parallel

1198
00:49:39.300 --> 00:49:42.210
to make sure that everybody who was there stayed there.

1199
00:49:42.210 --> 00:49:45.310
That was very important for them at the time.

1200
00:49:45.310 --> 00:49:47.560
But my grandmother needed to get to her husband.

1201
00:49:47.560 --> 00:49:52.100
And she had a newborn infant and he too was starving

1202
00:49:52.100 --> 00:49:56.430
and was very thirsty, and wouldn't stop crying.

1203
00:49:56.430 --> 00:49:59.840
It's very hard to sneak past the communist soldiers

1204
00:49:59.840 --> 00:50:02.310
when your baby is crying his eyeballs out.

1205
00:50:02.310 --> 00:50:07.070
So she went over to this dirty rice patty water

1206
00:50:07.070 --> 00:50:08.790
as my dad called it, the swamp water,

1207
00:50:08.790 --> 00:50:12.110
and like cupped it in her hands and brought it to his mouth

1208
00:50:12.110 --> 00:50:14.520
until he finally stopped crying.

1209
00:50:14.520 --> 00:50:17.490
And then she like sank all the way down

1210
00:50:17.490 --> 00:50:19.120
with her baby in her arms

1211
00:50:19.120 --> 00:50:22.040
until just their heads were, you know, above the water.

1212
00:50:22.040 --> 00:50:24.690
And they just slipped past the soldiers

1213
00:50:24.690 --> 00:50:27.350
and slipped past the 38th Parallel.

1214
00:50:27.350 --> 00:50:29.380
And then, according to my dad,

1215
00:50:29.380 --> 00:50:31.720
she spent much of the journey on foot

1216
00:50:31.720 --> 00:50:34.280
and encountered a tiger in the mountains

1217
00:50:34.280 --> 00:50:36.847
before finally meeting up with her husband.

1218
00:50:36.847 --> 00:50:40.030
And he had had another tiger encounter

1219
00:50:40.030 --> 00:50:41.163
prior to that, right?
Yes.

1220
00:50:41.163 --> 00:50:42.946
Like that's a recurring theme in his myth.

1221
00:50:42.946 --> 00:50:44.060
It is.

1222
00:50:44.060 --> 00:50:45.028
It is. And I love it.

1223
00:50:45.028 --> 00:50:46.347
In his hero's journey.

1224
00:50:46.347 --> 00:50:47.180
(both laugh)
It is.

1225
00:50:47.180 --> 00:50:50.150
So when my grandfather, when he was a little boy,

1226
00:50:50.150 --> 00:50:53.080
little boy in our point of view, he was 13.

1227
00:50:53.080 --> 00:50:56.040
But by that point, I guess he was considered a man.

1228
00:50:56.040 --> 00:50:59.540
He was 13 years old and he ran away from his home,

1229
00:50:59.540 --> 00:51:02.730
which was in plushy, South Korea, at the time.

1230
00:51:02.730 --> 00:51:05.750
He came from a fairly wealthy family.

1231
00:51:05.750 --> 00:51:08.580
And his father wanted him to marry somebody.

1232
00:51:08.580 --> 00:51:11.500
And he was like, "No, I don't want to marry this girl.

1233
00:51:11.500 --> 00:51:12.333
I don't know.

1234
00:51:12.333 --> 00:51:14.820
And oh, by the way, I think you're kind of a jerk."

1235
00:51:14.820 --> 00:51:18.010
And so he decided to run away from home at 13.

1236
00:51:18.010 --> 00:51:21.080
And he went to what is now known as North Korea.

1237
00:51:21.080 --> 00:51:23.060
And he had to cut through the mountains to do that.

1238
00:51:23.060 --> 00:51:24.520
And he did it largely on foot.

1239
00:51:24.520 --> 00:51:25.630
And according to legend,

1240
00:51:25.630 --> 00:51:28.356
he also encountered a tiger in the mountains.

1241
00:51:28.356 --> 00:51:30.330
Right. That's so wild.

1242
00:51:30.330 --> 00:51:31.870
It is. It is very wild.
It's crazy.

1243
00:51:31.870 --> 00:51:32.703
Yeah.

1244
00:51:32.703 --> 00:51:34.780
And my mom, when she was pregnant with my brother

1245
00:51:34.780 --> 00:51:36.450
had a dream about a tiger.

1246
00:51:36.450 --> 00:51:37.810
And that's how she knew she was pregnant.

1247
00:51:37.810 --> 00:51:38.940
Wow!

1248
00:51:38.940 --> 00:51:40.840
It's so funny to think about,

1249
00:51:40.840 --> 00:51:43.590
you know, your dad being this very quiet man,

1250
00:51:43.590 --> 00:51:45.250
but then suddenly breaking out

1251
00:51:45.250 --> 00:51:47.923
like just an insane story like that.

1252
00:51:47.923 --> 00:51:50.190
It's very typical of my dad.

1253
00:51:50.190 --> 00:51:53.092
His brain works a little differently, I think.

1254
00:51:53.092 --> 00:51:55.990
And you know, he loves his mother.

1255
00:51:55.990 --> 00:51:57.390
I mean, that's like the big thing

1256
00:51:57.390 --> 00:51:58.610
that I always get from my dad

1257
00:51:58.610 --> 00:52:02.500
is how much he loved his mother and I think it stems

1258
00:52:02.500 --> 00:52:05.180
from even just that, you know, origin story

1259
00:52:05.180 --> 00:52:07.190
of how she rescued him.

1260
00:52:07.190 --> 00:52:08.120
Yeah.

1261
00:52:08.120 --> 00:52:11.590
It's so heavy to think about the toil

1262
00:52:11.590 --> 00:52:15.423
and the difficulty of what that journey must have been like.

1263
00:52:16.537 --> 00:52:18.340
You know, it's almost impossible

1264
00:52:18.340 --> 00:52:21.490
that they made their way to the United States.

1265
00:52:21.490 --> 00:52:24.980
And now here you are, you know, the succeeding generation

1266
00:52:24.980 --> 00:52:25.813
to all of that.

1267
00:52:25.813 --> 00:52:27.960
And the more I think about that,

1268
00:52:27.960 --> 00:52:29.950
the more I think about all the people

1269
00:52:29.950 --> 00:52:31.590
that didn't make it across the border.

1270
00:52:31.590 --> 00:52:32.850
And there's some version of you

1271
00:52:32.850 --> 00:52:35.330
who's living in North Korea right now.

1272
00:52:35.330 --> 00:52:38.883
And, you know, the weight of that is,

1273
00:52:40.010 --> 00:52:42.550
you know, really difficult to grapple with.

1274
00:52:42.550 --> 00:52:43.453
But I think,

1275
00:52:44.450 --> 00:52:46.770
one of the reasons why I wanted you to share those stories

1276
00:52:46.770 --> 00:52:51.770
is it gives everything that you do that adequate ballast,

1277
00:52:52.520 --> 00:52:54.440
like there's a gravitas to it.

1278
00:52:54.440 --> 00:52:57.800
So in this three act structure of The Korean Vegan

1279
00:52:57.800 --> 00:53:00.250
with food blogger becomes,

1280
00:53:00.250 --> 00:53:01.930
you know, when Trump gets elected,

1281
00:53:01.930 --> 00:53:04.800
you have this impetus to start speaking about,

1282
00:53:04.800 --> 00:53:06.500
you know, things that are going on

1283
00:53:08.620 --> 00:53:10.700
in current events.

1284
00:53:10.700 --> 00:53:15.170
Your story lends so much weight to the discussion

1285
00:53:15.170 --> 00:53:19.040
about build the wall or, you know, kids in cages.

1286
00:53:19.040 --> 00:53:21.550
And how are we thinking about,

1287
00:53:21.550 --> 00:53:24.330
and talking about not just immigration,

1288
00:53:24.330 --> 00:53:26.690
but the immigrant experience

1289
00:53:26.690 --> 00:53:29.200
and everything that, you know, all the other issues

1290
00:53:29.200 --> 00:53:33.290
that are kind of tangential to that.

1291
00:53:33.290 --> 00:53:36.300
I think my parents' story

1292
00:53:36.300 --> 00:53:40.490
is so fundamental to who they are.

1293
00:53:40.490 --> 00:53:42.830
And as a result, who I am.

1294
00:53:42.830 --> 00:53:47.830
Like, I can't breathe a word without embedding their stories

1295
00:53:48.280 --> 00:53:50.090
in each of my words.

1296
00:53:50.090 --> 00:53:52.940
And so when I view things like news stories

1297
00:53:52.940 --> 00:53:55.100
about we're gonna build a wall,

1298
00:53:55.100 --> 00:53:58.040
or when I see children in cages,

1299
00:53:58.040 --> 00:54:01.660
I think about my mom saying we were refugees

1300
00:54:01.660 --> 00:54:03.830
and I was digging for sweet potatoes.

1301
00:54:03.830 --> 00:54:06.370
Like, how can I not think about that?

1302
00:54:06.370 --> 00:54:09.790
And, you know, when I think about,

1303
00:54:09.790 --> 00:54:12.910
you know people swarming the Capitol,

1304
00:54:12.910 --> 00:54:14.730
I have to think about my dad

1305
00:54:14.730 --> 00:54:19.730
and his sort of beautiful belief in democracy

1306
00:54:21.110 --> 00:54:24.430
and what that means and how much he dreamt

1307
00:54:24.430 --> 00:54:26.270
about coming to the United States,

1308
00:54:26.270 --> 00:54:29.200
because he believed in that beautiful dream.

1309
00:54:29.200 --> 00:54:31.880
So I can't like not think about those things

1310
00:54:31.880 --> 00:54:33.910
when I see this stuff on the news.

1311
00:54:33.910 --> 00:54:35.670
And so, yeah, I get emotional.

1312
00:54:35.670 --> 00:54:38.350
I cry when I see this stuff.

1313
00:54:38.350 --> 00:54:43.220
Because I think about what it means to me as who I am,

1314
00:54:43.220 --> 00:54:45.890
as Joanne, as the daughter of my parents.

1315
00:54:45.890 --> 00:54:47.350
Hmm.

1316
00:54:47.350 --> 00:54:50.210
But then there's the decision

1317
00:54:50.210 --> 00:54:52.590
to figure out a way to share that

1318
00:54:53.430 --> 00:54:56.090
in a manner that will connect with people.

1319
00:54:56.090 --> 00:54:59.430
It's one thing to, you know, go on Twitter

1320
00:54:59.430 --> 00:55:02.500
and shout and scream about how this is wrong,

1321
00:55:02.500 --> 00:55:04.770
or this person is racist.

1322
00:55:04.770 --> 00:55:08.630
Or I don't like what, you know, this leader is doing.

1323
00:55:08.630 --> 00:55:11.230
Versus telling a story

1324
00:55:11.230 --> 00:55:14.040
that's based on your own personal experience,

1325
00:55:14.040 --> 00:55:17.440
that engenders that emotional connection,

1326
00:55:17.440 --> 00:55:18.853
a sense of empathy.

1327
00:55:19.770 --> 00:55:24.770
And in so doing, I think shifts the lens on how people

1328
00:55:24.780 --> 00:55:26.490
are approaching issues like this.

1329
00:55:26.490 --> 00:55:27.700
Like, I think it's really powerful.

1330
00:55:27.700 --> 00:55:29.880
Like we need all kinds of activism

1331
00:55:29.880 --> 00:55:32.670
and all voices are important in that regard,

1332
00:55:32.670 --> 00:55:35.570
but I find personally the most powerful voice

1333
00:55:35.570 --> 00:55:37.950
is that person who's talented and gifted

1334
00:55:37.950 --> 00:55:41.730
enough to tell, you know, a really great story

1335
00:55:43.320 --> 00:55:46.260
that allows people to think about things

1336
00:55:46.260 --> 00:55:47.670
just a little bit differently.

1337
00:55:47.670 --> 00:55:50.400
I think that's the key. Just a little bit differently.

1338
00:55:50.400 --> 00:55:52.990
I'm not asking for a 180 turn.

1339
00:55:52.990 --> 00:55:55.610
I'm not even asking for a 45 degree angle.

1340
00:55:55.610 --> 00:55:57.910
I'm literally asking for a sliver

1341
00:55:57.910 --> 00:56:01.190
because that's the sliver that I can then get into

1342
00:56:01.190 --> 00:56:03.800
and maybe create a larger wedge

1343
00:56:03.800 --> 00:56:07.190
of sort of thinking and broadening.

1344
00:56:07.190 --> 00:56:10.110
And, you know, I was thinking about this

1345
00:56:10.110 --> 00:56:11.920
while I was actually reading your book this morning.

1346
00:56:11.920 --> 00:56:14.010
And I was like, you know, every time he says something

1347
00:56:14.010 --> 00:56:15.450
I'm like immediately invited

1348
00:56:15.450 --> 00:56:17.740
to be like, "How do I relate to that?"

1349
00:56:17.740 --> 00:56:19.500
And that's actually what I wanted to do

1350
00:56:19.500 --> 00:56:20.530
with The Korean Vegan,

1351
00:56:20.530 --> 00:56:24.070
is to create stories where a person is invited

1352
00:56:24.070 --> 00:56:25.700
to relate to it.

1353
00:56:25.700 --> 00:56:28.970
And then to reciprocate my vulnerability.

1354
00:56:28.970 --> 00:56:31.100
I'm sitting there putting myself out there.

1355
00:56:31.100 --> 00:56:34.010
And really what it is, is an invitation for them

1356
00:56:34.010 --> 00:56:36.530
to put themselves out there to me.

1357
00:56:36.530 --> 00:56:39.840
And that way sort of build this trust

1358
00:56:39.840 --> 00:56:43.800
that allows them to broaden their perspective.

1359
00:56:43.800 --> 00:56:44.800
Again, just a little bit.

1360
00:56:44.800 --> 00:56:48.734
Yeah. My friend Brogan calls it, moving fours to threes.

1361
00:56:48.734 --> 00:56:49.766
Oh, yeah! (laughs)

1362
00:56:49.766 --> 00:56:50.966
Which I think is great, right?

1363
00:56:50.966 --> 00:56:51.965
That's a great way of putting it.

1364
00:56:51.965 --> 00:56:52.900
In the spectrum of 10 or whatever,

1365
00:56:52.900 --> 00:56:55.610
or five to one, you know, being a spectrum

1366
00:56:55.610 --> 00:56:57.160
